-- js-model.rofl — the coverage audit, and nothing else yet.
--
-- THE ONE CLAIM THIS FILE EXISTS TO TEST: adding a semantic layer is ONE FACT,
-- and the audit immediately enumerates every cell that layer needs, with no
-- change to any rule and no code at all. If that holds the programme's premise
-- holds; if it needs an edit here, the premise is false and two languages is a
-- far cheaper place to learn it than twenty-one.
--
-- The audit is DECLARED as an audit ledger, not [main]: a coverage gap is a
-- statement about the model, not a fact of the domain.

-- THE BYPASS THIS REPLACES. The first version had `covered` include `ignored`,
-- so declaring the whole matrix ignored with plausible reasons drove the audit
-- to zero and it reported success: a discipline that says silence is not an
-- option, with a door one fact wide. Found by auditing my own tick rather than
-- by a test, an hour after stating the principle.
--
-- The repair is the form this ledger already uses for a dismissed finding:
-- an ignored cell is COUNTED AND VISIBLE, never hidden. Three buckets that
-- partition the matrix, and a waiver moves a cell between buckets rather than
-- out of sight.

modelled[audit](Lang, K, L)    :- handled(Lang, K, L, _).
waived[audit](Lang, K, L)      :- ignored(Lang, K, L, _).

unaccounted[audit](Lang, K, L) :- node_kind(Lang, K), layer(L),
                                  not modelled[audit](Lang, K, L),
                                  not waived[audit](Lang, K, L).

-- A cell claimed BOTH ways is a contradiction about the model, not a fact of
-- the domain, and it must be visible rather than resolved by rule order.
double_claimed[audit](Lang, K, L) :- modelled[audit](Lang, K, L),
                                     waived[audit](Lang, K, L).

-- AND A CLAIM ABOUT A CELL THAT DOES NOT EXIST. Found by making the mistake:
-- `handled(js, variable_declarator, dataflow, ...)` was recorded for a kind
-- absent from node_kind, the counts did not move, and NOTHING OBJECTED - the
-- claim simply evaporated. That is the same silence this audit exists to
-- forbid, in the audit itself. A claim naming an unknown kind or an unknown
-- layer is a typo or a gap in the vocabulary, and either way it must be said
-- out loud rather than dropped.
--
-- THE CHECK WAS WRITTEN THREE TIMES BY HAND BEFORE IT WAS WRITTEN ONCE. Four
-- arms for `handled` and `ignored`; then `unknown_because` arrived over the
-- same three arguments and the hole was open again — measured with a positive
-- control, the claim LOADS, is HELD in the store, and moves no count — and it
-- was closed by writing two more arms; then the shape ledger arrived and it
-- was open a third time. Nine arms over three ledgers, and the tenth ledger
-- reopens it. That is exactly the defect this file's own header names: the
-- gate's criterion is borrowed from whichever relation produced the first red.
--
-- THE REMEDY IS TO MAKE THE LEDGER NAME AN ARGUMENT. A claim about a cell is
-- ONE relation whose first argument says which ledger files it, so the
-- vocabulary check is one rule per ARITY instead of one pair per relation, and
-- a fourth ledger over the same arguments is checked the moment its facts are
-- spelled as claims — with no rule edit at all.
edb(claim).

-- WHICH LEDGERS EXIST, as rows, because the name is data now and data can be
-- misspelled. This is the hole the normalisation itself opens and it is closed
-- in the same breath: `claim(handeled, js, call_expression, dataflow, r)` is
-- not a claim about nothing, it is a claim nobody can read, and without this
-- table it would be audited for its CELL and pass.
edb(claim_kind).
claim_kind(handled).
claim_kind(ignored).
claim_kind(unknown_because).

-- ADOPTION and PROJECTION, and BOTH directions are wanted rather than one.
--
-- THE DIRECTION QUESTION, ANSWERED WITH A NUMBER. Sixty authored facts across
-- five files spell the three ledgers bare. Rewriting them into `claim(...)`
-- costs those 60 lines plus six projection rules and buys exactly ONE thing a
-- comment cannot — an example the next author copies. It buys NOTHING on the
-- property this block exists for: `claim` is extensional, so a fourth ledger
-- written as a claim reaches the audit with no rule either way. The facts
-- therefore stay where they are, and the two bridges below make the two
-- spellings interchangeable instead of making one of them canonical.
--
-- ADOPTION is legacy scaffolding, three lines wide, and it is precisely what a
-- fourth ledger does NOT need. PROJECTION is what stops a claim written in the
-- new spelling from HALF-working: without it `claim(handled, ...)` would be
-- audited and would tick nothing, which is a worse silence than the one this
-- block replaces, because it would look like it had been filed.
claim(handled,         Lang, K, L, R) :- handled(Lang, K, L, R).
claim(ignored,         Lang, K, L, R) :- ignored(Lang, K, L, R).
claim(unknown_because, Lang, K, L, R) :- unknown_because(Lang, K, L, R).

handled(Lang, K, L, R)         :- claim(handled, Lang, K, L, R).
ignored(Lang, K, L, R)         :- claim(ignored, Lang, K, L, R).
unknown_because(Lang, K, L, R) :- claim(unknown_because, Lang, K, L, R).

-- THE CHECK, ONCE. `not cell[audit]` rather than `not node_kind` and `not
-- layer` as separate arms: the coarse cell is exactly their conjunction, so
-- one premise covers both halves of the vocabulary and covers them in the one
-- place where the cell space is defined — which is the same move the shape
-- ledger's check already makes, arrived at from the other end.
orphan[audit](What, Lang, K, L) :- claim(What, Lang, K, L, _),
                                   not cell[audit](Lang, K, L).

unknown_ledger[audit](What) :- claim(What, _, _, _, _), not claim_kind(What).

-- ...and the name that was here before, now ONE projection of the check per
-- ledger rather than a copy of the check per ledger. Every query already
-- written against it answers the same rows.
orphan_claim[audit](Lang, K, L)  :- orphan[audit](handled, Lang, K, L).
orphan_claim[audit](Lang, K, L)  :- orphan[audit](ignored, Lang, K, L).

-- the cells that exist at all, so the audit can be read as a fraction rather
-- than as a bare count
cell[audit](Lang, K, L)        :- node_kind(Lang, K), layer(L).

-- WHERE THE LANGUAGES AGREE — and the first version of this was a TAUTOLOGY.
-- It read `handled(A, _, L, Rule), handled(B, _, L, Rule), A != B` and reported
-- eight rows of convergence, of which all four named rules EXISTED NOWHERE: I
-- had typed the same atom into two handled facts and the relation called it
-- agreement. A derived relation that looks like a finding and reflects only the
-- keyboard.
--
-- Convergence now requires EVIDENCE on both sides: the cell must have been run
-- against an oracle, which is what `checked` records. Nothing is checked yet, so
-- this correctly reports ZERO, and it will grow only as work is actually done.
--   checked(Lang, Kind, Layer, Rule, Oracle, Fixtures)
edb(checked).

verified[audit](Lang, K, L, Rule)  :- handled(Lang, K, L, Rule),
                                      checked(Lang, K, L, Rule, _, _).

converges[audit](L, Rule, A, B)    :- verified[audit](A, _, L, Rule),
                                      verified[audit](B, _, L, Rule), A != B.

-- and the gap between claiming and showing, which is the number that matters
unverified[audit](Lang, K, L, Rule) :- handled(Lang, K, L, Rule),
                                       not checked(Lang, K, L, Rule, _, _).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE DEFAULT VERDICT — «по дефолту ставим "не смоделировано"», like an
-- abstract base class: declared, answering when asked, and waiting for a
-- concrete implementation to be supplied.
--
-- Everything above this line answers only where somebody already wrote
-- something down. `modelled` needs a `handled` fact, `waived` needs an
-- `ignored` one, and `unaccounted` — the one relation that speaks for a cell
-- nobody has touched — speaks by ABSENCE: it is named for what is missing, so
-- the reading of an empty result is "nothing is missing" and the reading of a
-- silent model is the same picture. That is the standing rule of this
-- repository turned on this file: an empty answer is a fact about the QUERY
-- until proven otherwise.
--
-- A total function over the cell space fixes it. Every cell carries a verdict;
-- `not_modelled` is the one it is born with. It is a positive, typed,
-- queryable row, so `why verdict[audit](js, yield_expression, dataflow,
-- not_modelled)` is askable and answers with the two failing premises — where
-- the absence of an `unaccounted` row could only ever be approached from the
-- other side, with `whynot`.
--
-- The first two rules deliberately do NOT carry `cell[audit]` as a premise:
-- they mirror `modelled`/`waived` exactly, which is what makes the row-for-row
-- agreement with `unaccounted` a real cross-check rather than a restatement.
-- The consequence is measured and wanted: a `handled` fact naming a kind that
-- does not exist produces a verdict with no cell under it, and the partition
-- count breaks — a SECOND, independent detector for the thing `orphan_claim`
-- reports.
verdict[audit](Lang, K, L, modelled)     :- handled(Lang, K, L, _).
verdict[audit](Lang, K, L, waived)       :- ignored(Lang, K, L, _).
verdict[audit](Lang, K, L, not_modelled) :- cell[audit](Lang, K, L),
                                            not handled(Lang, K, L, _),
                                            not ignored(Lang, K, L, _).

-- THE TYPE OF THE UNKNOWN, which is what turns a count into a queue. "247
-- unknowns" is a number nobody can act on; "3 irreducible, 244 ours" is a work
-- queue with a bottom and an owner. The four are not equal — exactly ONE of
-- them is a property of the SUBJECT rather than of us:
edb(unknown_type).
unknown_type(runtime_dependent, irreducible).
unknown_type(not_yet,           ours).
unknown_type(budget_exhausted,  ours).
unknown_type(out_of_scope,      ours).

-- The authored reason, per cell. Host data like `checked`: nobody derives it,
-- and without the declaration `undefined_premise` would read the empty table
-- as a misspelling.   unknown_because(Lang, Kind, Layer, Reason)
edb(unknown_because).

-- ...and the DEFAULT that makes it total. A not_modelled cell that nobody has
-- explained is `not_yet` — ours, unfinished, on the queue. This is the second
-- place the abstract-class shape appears: the base answer exists, and an
-- authored fact overrides it.
reason[audit](Lang, K, L, R)       :- verdict[audit](Lang, K, L, not_modelled),
                                      unknown_because(Lang, K, L, R).
reason[audit](Lang, K, L, not_yet) :- verdict[audit](Lang, K, L, not_modelled),
                                      not unknown_because(Lang, K, L, _).

irreducible_unknown[audit](Lang, K, L) :- reason[audit](Lang, K, L, R),
                                          unknown_type(R, irreducible).
our_unknown[audit](Lang, K, L)         :- reason[audit](Lang, K, L, R),
                                          unknown_type(R, ours).

-- TWO AUDITS OF THE AUDIT, and both must be able to say no.
--
-- A reason whose atom is in no taxonomy is not a reason, it is a word. It also
-- falls out of BOTH counts above, so the split stops summing to the
-- not_modelled total — the silent half of this defect, and the reason the test
-- asserts the sum rather than the two counts alone.
bad_reason[audit](Lang, K, L, R)   :- unknown_because(Lang, K, L, R),
                                      not unknown_type(R, _).

-- An excuse that outlived its cause: the cell was modelled or waived since,
-- and the reason stayed behind. Narrow on purpose — it demands the cell EXIST,
-- so a reason naming a kind nobody declared is not laundered into this
-- relation under a name that would read as "we fixed that one".
stale_reason[audit](Lang, K, L, R) :- unknown_because(Lang, K, L, R),
                                      cell[audit](Lang, K, L),
                                      not verdict[audit](Lang, K, L, not_modelled).

-- ...which is where the hole `orphan_claim` was written for used to reopen, in
-- the new relation. MEASURED before it was closed: with the two hand-written
-- rules that stood here stripped, an `unknown_because(js, no_such_kind,
-- dataflow, not_yet)` moved no count, fired no audit, and evaporated exactly
-- as `handled(js, variable_declarator, ...)` once did.
--
-- IT NO LONGER REOPENS, and the difference is worth stating in the same place
-- the defect was: `unknown_because` is ADOPTED as a claim above, so the single
-- `orphan[audit]` rule already objects to it, and stripping this line leaves
-- the objection standing under its general name. What is left here is one
-- PROJECTION, so every query already written against the old name answers the
-- same rows it did.
orphan_reason[audit](Lang, K, L) :- orphan[audit](unknown_because, Lang, K, L).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE THIRD AXIS — SHAPE — AND WHY ITS APPLICABILITY IS A FACT
--
-- WHAT PAID FOR IT. `member_expression` is ONE KIND and SIX DIFFERENT JOBS —
-- `o.m()`, `a.b.c()`, `f().g()`, `this.x()`, `super.x()`, `o[k]()` — nine
-- distinct shapes measured over the call corpus and thirteen the grammar
-- allows. The two-axis matrix carries ONE tick for all of them,
-- `handled(js, member_expression, callgraph, r_call_edge)`, and the execution
-- oracle found 37 edges where the model found 26. SIX of the eleven misses sit
-- inside that single ticked cell. The table was built so that an EMPTY cell
-- would shout; the hole turned out to be INSIDE a full one, where a table
-- keyed by kind is silent by construction.
--
-- THE ARITY IS THE POINT, and it is also the honest limit. A relation here has
-- a fixed arity, so an AXIS is a COLUMN and adding a column is a RULE edit.
-- What is a fact — and what this section exists to make a fact — is
--   * which VALUES the axis takes for a kind      shape_of/3
--   * which LAYERS the axis applies to            axis_applies/2
--   * that the axis exists at all                 axis/1
-- `layer(scope).` really does add a whole dimension in one fact. `axis(shape).`
-- does not, and claiming otherwise would be the tautology this file has already
-- paid for once.
--
-- WHY APPLICABILITY IS DECLARED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Measured on the tick
-- before this one: the call-graph model named 24 node kinds the matrix did not
-- know, and at two layers that was exactly 48 new cells, NOT ONE of which was a
-- question. Pure arithmetic. A LOCAL axis multiplied over layers where it means
-- nothing repeats that exactly: a callee's shape is a fact about a call and
-- says nothing whatever about a module graph. So applicability is a ROW, the
-- row can be wrong, and `unearned_axis[audit]` is the relation that says so.
edb(axis).
edb(axis_applies).
edb(shape_of).
-- WHICH LAYER A REFINEMENT VALUE BELONGS TO. Added 2026-09-04, and the reason
-- is a defect rather than a design: `import_declaration x modules` was `handled`
-- AND carried two live excuses — the rule resolves a relative specifier while a
-- bare one is out of scope and a subpath one is not done — so `stale_reason`
-- reported three FALSE rows in the only world that holds all the fact packs.
-- The cell is PARTIAL and a matrix keyed by kind cannot say so. That is the
-- member_expression limit arriving in a second layer.
--
-- ONE COLUMN, PER-LAYER VOCABULARIES. A callee shape really does say nothing
-- about a module graph, exactly as facts/js-shapes.rofl argues — so the column
-- is shared and its VALUES are not. `shape_in` is what stops a specifier shape
-- minting a phantom cell in the call graph, and it is why declaring the modules
-- refinement costs one premise rather than a fifth argument.
edb(shape_in).

-- A kind the axis SPLITS. A kind with no declared shape is ATOMIC for this
-- axis even in a layer where the axis applies, and it keeps exactly one cell.
-- Without this the refinement would delete `if_statement x callgraph` — a cell
-- somebody deliberately waived — from the table entirely.
shape_kind[audit](Lang, K, Lay) :- shape_of(Lang, K, S), shape_in(S, Lay).

-- THE CELL, AT THREE AXES. Same name, one more argument. This kernel keys a
-- relation by NAME and unifies by ARITY, so `cell[audit]/3` above keeps
-- answering every query already written against it while `cell[audit]/4` is
-- the same matrix refined; the two are cross-checked against each other by
-- `lost_cell` and `invented_cell` below rather than trusted to agree.
--
-- `none` is not a shape. It is the reading "this cell is not refined by the
-- axis", and there are TWO different ways to earn it — the axis does not apply
-- to the layer, or it applies and this kind does not split. Collapsing them
-- into one rule would make the second unremovable and therefore untestable.
cell[audit](Lang, K, none, Lay)  :- node_kind(Lang, K), layer(Lay),
                                    not axis_applies(shape, Lay).
cell[audit](Lang, K, none, Lay)  :- node_kind(Lang, K), layer(Lay),
                                    axis_applies(shape, Lay),
                                    not shape_kind[audit](Lang, K, Lay).
cell[audit](Lang, K, Shape, Lay) :- shape_of(Lang, K, Shape), shape_in(Shape, Lay),
                                    axis_applies(shape, Lay).

-- THE REFINEMENT IS A PARTITION OF THE COARSE MATRIX, and both directions are
-- checked. A coarse cell with no fine cell under it has been LOST by the
-- refinement; a fine cell over no coarse cell has been INVENTED, which is the
-- vocabulary hole `orphan_claim` was written for, arriving through a third
-- relation over the same arguments exactly as this file predicted it would.
coarse_of[audit](Lang, K, Lay)     :- cell[audit](Lang, K, _, Lay).
lost_cell[audit](Lang, K, Lay)     :- cell[audit](Lang, K, Lay),
                                      not coarse_of[audit](Lang, K, Lay).
invented_cell[audit](Lang, K, Lay) :- coarse_of[audit](Lang, K, Lay),
                                      not cell[audit](Lang, K, Lay).

-- ...and a cell must not be BOTH refined and unrefined. Stated as the
-- invariant rather than copied from the guard above: one coarse cell is
-- either split by the axis or it is not, so `none` beside a real shape is a
-- cell counted twice.
refined_cell[audit](Lang, K, Lay) :- cell[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay), S != none.
double_cell[audit](Lang, K, Lay)  :- cell[audit](Lang, K, none, Lay),
                                     refined_cell[audit](Lang, K, Lay).

-- A shape declared for a kind nobody declared, and an applicability declared
-- for an axis or a layer nobody declared. The same silence, in the two new
-- relations.
orphan_shape[audit](Lang, K, S) :- shape_of(Lang, K, S), not node_kind(Lang, K).
orphan_axis[audit](A, Lay)      :- axis_applies(A, Lay), not axis(A).
orphan_axis[audit](A, Lay)      :- axis_applies(A, Lay), not layer(Lay).

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE CLAIM LEDGER AT SHAPE GRANULARITY
--
-- Authored facts carry a shape:  handled(Lang, K, Shape, Layer, Rule)
--                                ignored(Lang, K, Shape, Layer, Reason)
--                                unknown_because(Lang, K, Shape, Layer, Reason)
--
-- A KIND-LEVEL CLAIM ANSWERS ONLY FOR AN UNREFINED CELL. This is the whole
-- content of the axis: `handled(js, member_expression, callgraph, r_call_edge)`
-- does NOT tick thirteen shapes, it ticks nothing, because the cell it names
-- no longer exists. The claim is still true of SOMETHING — the rule really
-- does resolve `ns.method()` — which is why it is reported by
-- `coarser_claim[audit]` rather than deleted or silently honoured.
shaped_handled[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, R)    :- handled(Lang, K, S, Lay, R).
shaped_handled[audit](Lang, K, none, Lay, R) :- handled(Lang, K, Lay, R),
                                                cell[audit](Lang, K, none, Lay).
shaped_ignored[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, R)    :- ignored(Lang, K, S, Lay, R).
shaped_ignored[audit](Lang, K, none, Lay, R) :- ignored(Lang, K, Lay, R),
                                                cell[audit](Lang, K, none, Lay).
shaped_because[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, R)    :- unknown_because(Lang, K, S, Lay, R).
shaped_because[audit](Lang, K, none, Lay, R) :- unknown_because(Lang, K, Lay, R),
                                                cell[audit](Lang, K, none, Lay).

coarser_claim[audit](Lang, K, Lay, R) :- handled(Lang, K, Lay, R),
                                         shape_kind[audit](Lang, K, Lay),
                                         axis_applies(shape, Lay).
coarser_claim[audit](Lang, K, Lay, R) :- ignored(Lang, K, Lay, R),
                                         shape_kind[audit](Lang, K, Lay),
                                         axis_applies(shape, Lay).

-- THE DEFAULT SURVIVES THE REFINEMENT, which is the one property that may not
-- be traded for the extra column: every cell of the three-axis matrix carries
-- a verdict and `not_modelled` is the one it is born with. As above, the
-- AUTHORED branch of `shaped_handled` deliberately does not carry
-- `cell[audit]`, so a shape claim over no cell produces a verdict with no cell
-- under it and the partition stops summing — a second, independent detector
-- for what `orphan_shape` reports.
verdict[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, modelled)     :- shaped_handled[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, _).
verdict[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, waived)       :- shaped_ignored[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, _).
verdict[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, not_modelled) :- cell[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay),
                                                 not shaped_handled[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, _),
                                                 not shaped_ignored[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, _).

double_claimed[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay) :- verdict[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, modelled),
                                          verdict[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, waived).

-- A CLAIM ABOUT A CELL THAT DOES NOT EXIST, at the finer grain. It is derived
-- from the CELL rather than from the vocabulary tables, so it covers every way
-- a claim can miss at once — an undeclared kind, an undeclared shape, an
-- unknown layer, and the one the vocabulary checks cannot see, a shape claim
-- in a layer the axis does not apply to.
--
-- AND IT IS THE SAME RULE AS THE COARSE ONE, at one more argument. The ledger
-- name is an argument here too, so the three ledgers cost three ADOPTION lines
-- and the check itself is written once for this arity. The honest limit is
-- visible in that sentence: the check is generic over the LEDGER and not over
-- the ARITY, because a relation has a fixed arity here, so a fourth axis would
-- need `claim/7` and one more copy of these two rules — which is the same
-- honest limit `axis(shape)` has, and it is stated in the same terms.
claim(handled,         Lang, K, S, Lay, R) :- handled(Lang, K, S, Lay, R).
claim(ignored,         Lang, K, S, Lay, R) :- ignored(Lang, K, S, Lay, R).
claim(unknown_because, Lang, K, S, Lay, R) :- unknown_because(Lang, K, S, Lay, R).

handled(Lang, K, S, Lay, R)         :- claim(handled, Lang, K, S, Lay, R).
ignored(Lang, K, S, Lay, R)         :- claim(ignored, Lang, K, S, Lay, R).
unknown_because(Lang, K, S, Lay, R) :- claim(unknown_because, Lang, K, S, Lay, R).

orphan[audit](What, Lang, K, S, Lay) :- claim(What, Lang, K, S, Lay, _),
                                        not cell[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay).

unknown_ledger[audit](What) :- claim(What, _, _, _, _, _), not claim_kind(What).

orphan_claim[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay)  :- orphan[audit](handled, Lang, K, S, Lay).
orphan_claim[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay)  :- orphan[audit](ignored, Lang, K, S, Lay).
orphan_reason[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay) :- orphan[audit](unknown_because, Lang, K, S, Lay).

reason[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, R)       :- verdict[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, not_modelled),
                                           shaped_because[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, R).
reason[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, not_yet) :- verdict[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, not_modelled),
                                           not shaped_because[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, _).

bad_reason[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, R) :- unknown_because(Lang, K, S, Lay, R),
                                         not unknown_type(R, _).
irreducible_unknown[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay) :- reason[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, R),
                                               unknown_type(R, irreducible).
our_unknown[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay)         :- reason[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, R),
                                               unknown_type(R, ours).

-- ===========================================================================
-- CAN ANYTHING SAY NO TO A LOCAL AXIS DECLARED AS CROSS-CUTTING?
--
-- Yes, and this is the relation that does it. An axis EARNS a layer when its
-- refinement makes the model say something DIFFERENT about two shapes of the
-- SAME kind there — a different verdict, or a different typed reason. An axis
-- that refines a layer into rows all carrying the same answer has multiplied
-- the table and answered nothing, which is precisely the 48-cells-no-questions
-- measurement above wearing a new column.
--
-- The comparison is per KIND on purpose. Two different kinds disagreeing says
-- only that the matrix already had two rows; the axis is earning its column
-- exactly when it splits ONE row into rows that disagree.
refined_verdict[audit](Lang, K, Lay, V) :- verdict[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, V), S != none.
refined_reason[audit](Lang, K, Lay, R)  :- reason[audit](Lang, K, S, Lay, R), S != none.

axis_earns[audit](A, Lay)    :- axis(A),
                                refined_verdict[audit](Lang, K, Lay, V),
                                refined_verdict[audit](Lang, K, Lay, W), V != W.
axis_earns[audit](A, Lay)    :- axis(A),
                                refined_reason[audit](Lang, K, Lay, R1),
                                refined_reason[audit](Lang, K, Lay, R2), R1 != R2.
unearned_axis[audit](A, Lay) :- axis_applies(A, Lay), not axis_earns[audit](A, Lay).

-- ============ what this layer's ledgers are allowed to read ============
--
-- Written out because the kernel stopped writing it for us. `crossing` used
-- to carry `not bridge_decl(R, A, B)` and `src/reflect.ts` emitted that row
-- for any rule whose head named a ledger and whose body read another — so
-- every rule here licensed its own read by existing, and `leak[audit]` was 0
-- across this whole layer for a reason that had nothing to do with the layer.
-- With that gone the layer reports 6 crossings and declares them here.
--
-- [code] READS [main]. Twelve rules do it — `obj_class`, `call_site`,
-- `shape_known`, `explicit_shape` and the rest — because the kind and shape
-- tables they join against are ordinary program facts with no ledger of their
-- own. The scanner's book is built from them; that is what this layer is.
imports(code, main).

-- [audit] READS [code]. Twenty-nine rules: every gate in this layer reads the
